 "standard_code": "Year 4: Multiplication and division",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Recall multiplication and division facts for multiplication tables up to 12 × 12.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 4 times tables worksheets for the Multiplication Tables Check: arrays, tricks for the 6, 7, 8, 9, 11 and 12 times tables, division facts and word problems. 100 questions with an answer key, following the National Curriculum.",
 "key_big": "Learn a trick for every hard table: × 9 is × 10 take away one group, and × 12 is × 10 plus × 2.",
 "key_sub": "Every fact gives you two more: 7 × 8 = 56 means 8 × 7 = 56, 56 ÷ 7 = 8 and 56 ÷ 8 = 7. Know them all up to 12 × 12 by the end of Year 4.",
 "body_html": "<h2>Multiplication is equal groups</h2><p><b>6 × 4</b> means 6 lots of 4. In an array, that is 6 rows with 4 dots in each row. Turn the array on its side and you get 4 × 6. The answer is the same: 24. So when you learn one fact, you know two.</p><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 132 192' width='132' height='192' role='img' aria-label='array of 6 rows with 4 dots in each row'><g font-family='Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'><circle cx='21' cy='21' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='21' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='21' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='21' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='21' cy='51' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='51' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='51' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='51' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='21' cy='81' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='81' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='81' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='81' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='21' cy='111' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='111' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='111' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='111' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='21' cy='141' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='141' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='141' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='141' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='21' cy='171' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='51' cy='171' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='81' cy='171' r='10' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='111' cy='171' r='10' fill='#006633'/></g></svg><figcaption>6 rows of 4: 6 × 4 = 24. The same array shows 4 × 6 = 24, 24 ÷ 6 = 4 and 24 ÷ 4 = 6.</figcaption></figure><h2>The Multiplication Tables Check</h2><p>By the end of Year 4, the National Curriculum says pupils should know all the times tables up to <b>12 × 12</b>. In June of Year 4, children in England take the <b>Multiplication Tables Check (MTC)</b>. It is an online check of <b>25 questions</b> from the 2 to 12 times tables. Each question has <b>6 seconds</b> to type the answer. There is no pass mark: it shows which facts a child knows quickly.</p><h2>Tricks for the hardest tables</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Trick</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>× 6</td><td>× 5, then add one more group</td><td>7 × 6 = 35 + 7 = 42</td></tr><tr><td>× 7</td><td>× 5 plus × 2</td><td>8 × 7 = 40 + 16 = 56</td></tr><tr><td>× 8</td><td>Double, double, double</td><td>6 × 8: 12, 24, 48</td></tr><tr><td>× 9</td><td>× 10, then take one group away</td><td>7 × 9 = 70 − 7 = 63</td></tr><tr><td>× 11</td><td>Up to 11 × 9, write the digit twice</td><td>11 × 4 = 44</td></tr><tr><td>× 12</td><td>× 10 plus × 2</td><td>12 × 7 = 70 + 14 = 84</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 310 222' width='310' height='222' role='img' aria-label='7 rows of 12 dots split into 10 and 2 columns'><g font-family='Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'><circle cx='18' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='38' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='62' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='86' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='110' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='134' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='158' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='18' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='42' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='66' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='90' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='114' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='138' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='162' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='186' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='210' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='234' cy='182' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='268' cy='38' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='38' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='62' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='62' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='86' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='86' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='110' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='110' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='134' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='134' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='158' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='158' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='268' cy='182' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><circle cx='292' cy='182' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><line x1='251' y1='22' x2='251' y2='198' stroke='#2b2b2b' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='5 4'/><text x='126.0' y='18.0' font-size='16' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>10</text><text x='280.0' y='18.0' font-size='16' font-weight='700' fill='#c0392b' text-anchor='middle'>2</text><text x='126.0' y='215.0' font-size='16' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>10 × 7 = 70</text><text x='280.0' y='215.0' font-size='16' font-weight='700' fill='#c0392b' text-anchor='middle'>14</text></g></svg><figcaption>12 × 7: split 12 into 10 and 2. 10 × 7 = 70 and 2 × 7 = 14. 70 + 14 = 84.</figcaption></figure><p>In the 9 times table, the digits add up to 9: 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90. This is a quick way to check an answer.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>8 × 7 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5 × 7 = 35 and 3 × 7 = 21. 35 + 21 = 56. Or double 7 three times: 14, 28, 56.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 56</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Division</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>63 ÷ 9 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Division is the inverse of multiplication. Think: 9 × ? = 63. 9 × 7 = 63.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 7</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Word problem</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Eggs come in boxes of 12. How many eggs are in 6 boxes?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 lots of 12: 10 × 6 = 60 and 2 × 6 = 12. 60 + 12 = 72.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 72 eggs</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mixing up a neighbouring fact: 7 × 8 = 54 or 48</td><td>Use a trick: 7 × 8 = 35 + 21 = 56</td></tr><tr><td>Adding instead: 7 × 8 = 15</td><td>× means lots of: 7 lots of 8</td></tr><tr><td>Swapping the digits: 12 × 7 = 48</td><td>Split it: 70 + 14 = 84. Say the answer out loud</td></tr><tr><td>One group too few: 8 × 6 = 40</td><td>Count on from a fact you know: 5 × 8 = 40, then 40 + 8 = 48</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the Multiplication Tables Check?",
   "a": "It is a statutory online check that Year 4 pupils in England take in June. There are 25 multiplication questions from the 2 to 12 times tables, with 6 seconds to answer each one."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is there a pass mark for the Multiplication Tables Check?",
   "a": "No. It is a check, not a test you pass or fail. Each child gets a score out of 25, and schools use it to see which pupils need more help with their times tables."
  },
  {
   "q": "What times tables should a Year 4 child know?",
   "a": "All of them up to 12 × 12. The National Curriculum expects pupils to recall multiplication and division facts for every table from 2 to 12 by the end of Year 4."
  },
  {
   "q": "Which times tables are the hardest to learn?",
   "a": "Most children find the 6, 7, 8, 9, 11 and 12 times tables hardest. Learn a trick for each, such as × 9 = × 10 take away one group, then practise a few facts every day."
  }
 ],
